# HQL extensions and namespace initialization

Extensions provide capabilities outside the small [[hql/core]]. They may be
implemented in Rust, but HQL users should interact with ordinary typed functions
and values. Rust traits, lifetimes and implementation details do not become HQL
language requirements.

## The registry

A registry of extensions is implemented; a module loader is not. An extension
declares a name, a version and its steps, and each step supplies its
evaluator, its own marking of whether it is pure, and, where no declaration
states one, a structural `StepSpec` — so a capability is added without
editing the core's checker or evaluator. Extensions
are compiled into the binary and registered in one table. There is no dynamic
loading and no plugin ABI: registration says what exists, and it is a host
mechanism that never reaches HQL's surface.

The signature declares generic parameters and bounds, input and result types,
argument names and arity, and lambda or type arguments. The checker and
`hql builtins` consume the same declaration. Ordinary steps need no custom
checker; dependent hooks add constraints such as intrinsic ordering or a
configured semantic index. Invalid signatures are rejected before checking a
program. Unknown, duplicate, and excess arguments are errors rather than ignored
syntax. Evaluators receive the checked execution tree, with resolved step and
type identities.

Registration is not import. Every extension in the binary is registered;
a program resolves what it has imported. That is what lets a diagnostic name
the import a program is missing instead of guessing at a misspelling.

## How an HQL interface is wired to its implementation

**An HQL module is a language contract. Extensions do not extend the HQL type
system; they provide native implementations for declarations already present
in that contract.** An extension may implement declarations; it may not create
them: that a function exists and its signature, that a type exists with its
parents and fields, that a type implements a trait, are all declared in HQL. A
module needs no extension at all when its bodies are HQL, and may mix HQL and
native bodies. The model and its stages are
[HQL-0024 part 12](../../proposals/HQL-0024/parts/12-contracts-and-binding.md).

| In the `.hql` contract | In the extension | Matched by |
| --- | --- | --- |
| the module, `std/string.hql` is `string` | the descriptor's `provides: &["string"]` | the canonical module name; the claim is total: every external declaration of the module is implemented, and nothing else is |
| an external declaration, `fn trim(self) : String` | an operation named `trim`: `declared("trim", summary, eval_trim)` | the name |
| one name declared more than once, `fn take<S : Seq<T>, T>(self: S, n: Int) : S` then `fn take<T>(self: Stream<T>, n: Int) : List<T>` | one operation, whose one implementation serves every overload | the name; a call takes the first overload its input and arguments fit |
| the receiver, `self`, typed by the module's pattern or written, `self: Collection<T>`, `self: Int` | the operation's input | binding derives it from the declaration; `value.f()` reaches only a declaration with `self`, and every form of one name has a receiver or none does |
| the purity, `fn` pure and `effectful fn source(…)` effectful | the operation's own marking, `Purity::Effectful` | binding refuses a marking that differs; checking reads the declaration |
| the shape, `elementwise fn`, `partition fn`, `reduction fn`, and a plain `fn` a barrier | the operation's own marking, `Shape::Elementwise` | binding refuses a marking that differs; `hql builtins` and a planner read the declaration |
| the generic parameters and their bounds, a parameter bound by a shape, `S : Seq<T>`, which is the input at its own type, and a constructor parameter, `F<_> : Iterable<_>` or `M<_, _> : Map<_, _>`, which `F<U>` rebuilds around other arguments; the parameters in order, a function type as a lambda, `T -> Is<U>` as a predicate testing its parameter's type, `...T` as a variadic argument of one type, `...Any` as one of any type in each position, `step: Int = 1` as one that may be omitted, the parameters before `/` as supplied by position only and those after `*` by name only | the operation's arguments | binding derives them from the declaration |
| the result type | the operation's output | binding derives it from the declaration |
| a native implementation, `impl Ord for Int`, `impl<T> Index<T> for Seq<T>` | a body for the trait's functions over the type's values | the trait and the type; a header needs a body and a body needs a header |
| a codec, `impl Decoder<String> for Lines`, `impl<T> Encoder<T> for Json` | the decoder and encoder tables | the format: a format read has the one decoder its header names, and a format writes a type exactly when an encoder header covers it |
| a declared type, `type File : {Replayable, Writable} { path : String }` | a `Value` representation and the constructor that builds it, `file(path)` | the type's name; the extension adds no type, parent or field |

The declaration is the signature. An operation implementing one writes no
type in Rust: binding reads each declaration of its name and gives the
checker the signatures they state, in the order written. An operation whose
result is computed from the call, what `source` reads from its endpoint and
format, keeps a native signature; binding compares it with the declaration in
full, the input, each argument's name and type, the bounds and the result,
and refuses a native operation whose name is declared more than once. Binding
and validation run once per runtime environment, the module graph and the
extension set, before the first program; the validated environment is frozen
and shared by every program run against it, and a disagreement refuses every
program with a message naming the module, the declaration and the extension.

An overloaded name is checked against one declaration per call: the first
whose input the call's input meets, whose required arguments are all
supplied, whose function arguments take as many parameters as the functions
written, and whose value arguments take the types written. More specific
declarations come first, and binding refuses a declaration that an earlier
one of the name always takes first:

```hql
// std/collections.hql
fn sort<F<_> : Seq<_>, T : Orderable>(self: F<T>) : F<T>
fn sort<T : Orderable>(self: Collection<T>) : List<T>
fn sort<F<_> : Seq<_>, T, K : Orderable>(self: F<T>, by: T -> K) : F<T>
fn sort<T, K : Orderable>(self: Collection<T>, by: T -> K) : List<T>
fn sort<F<_> : Seq<_>, T>(self: F<T>, by: (T, T) -> Ordering) : F<T>
fn sort<T>(self: Collection<T>, by: (T, T) -> Ordering) : List<T>
```

`[3, 1] | sort` is a `List<Int>`, `{3, 1} | sort` a `List<Int>`, and a
`Ranking<Card>` sorted by a key a `Seq<Hit<Card>>`, its constructor rebuilt
around its element. Where no declaration fits, the refusal names the inputs
the declarations take: `` `sum` needs Collection<Int> or Collection<Float>, not
List<String> ``. Where a call fits a declaration by its arity and none by
its arguments' types, and another declaration takes those arguments, the
refusal names every declaration of that arity: `2 | pow(2.0)` names
`Int | pow(exponent: Int)` and `Float | pow(exponent: Float)`.

The parameters before a `/` are supplied by position only, and those after
a `*` by name only; a function with a body takes every argument by position,
so both belong to an external declaration:

```hql
// std/collections.hql, std/format.hql
fn count<T>(self: Collection<T>, value: T, /) : Int
fn format<T, F : Format>(x: T, as: Type<F>, *, limit: Int = 0, renderer: String = "") : String
```

`[1, 2, 1] | count(1)` is `2`, and `count(value = 1)` is refused.

A predicate whose type is `T -> Is<U>` takes only a lambda that tests its
parameter's type, `x => x | is(U)`, and binds `U` to the type tested, so the
narrowing `filter` is a declaration of its own, written before the general
one:

```hql
fn filter<F<_> : Iterable<_>, T, U>(self: F<T>, by: T -> Is<U>) : F<U>
fn filter<S : Iterable<T>, T>(self: S, by: T -> Bool) : S
```

A method call, a qualified call and a pipeline step reach the same
declaration:

```hql
// std/string.hql: a receiver module over String; every function is external.
module for String
fn trim(self) : String
fn pad_left(self, width: Int, fill: String) : String
```

`"x".pad_left(3, "0")`, `string.pad_left("x", 3, "0")` and
`"x" | pad_left(3, "0")` resolve the declaration `string.pad_left`, then run
the `string` extension's operation bound to it; the checked program carries
the declaration's identity. `text | parse` lowering to `parse(text)` is a
property of the pipeline syntax, not of the module: a declaration without
`self` has no receiver, so `range(1, 3)` and `1 | range(3)` reach
`fn range(start: Int, …)` and `1.range(3)` is refused. Loading `std/`
allocates an identity for each module and each external declaration, and
binding maps each declaration identity to the operation implementing it.

A trait the language owns, and the built-in types' implementations of it, are
declarations of `std/core.hql`:

```hql
trait Ord {
    fn compare(self, other: Self) : Ordering
}
impl Ord for Int
impl Ord for Float
impl Ord for String
```

The header is the whole HQL side of a native implementation; the runtime
binds the body. `impl Ord for Version { … }` in a program is an HQL body.
Both are entries of one implementation table, and `compare`, `<`, `sort`,
`min` and `max` resolve through it alike. A built-in type implements exactly
the traits its headers name: `String` is `Ord` and not `Add`, `Bool` is `Eq`
and not `Ord`.

Implementations are coherent: no two of one trait overlap at one type and one
set of arguments, across the standard library, imported modules and the
program, the error naming both; an
implementation is declared with its trait or with its type, so a program
implements its own trait for `Int` and `Eq` for its own `Version`, and never
`Add` for `String`. A bodyless header belongs to a module an extension
provides; a program writes the functions in braces.

Four parts of the model are not yet built. An operation whose result is
computed from the call carries a native signature beside its declaration,
compared rather than replaced. Identities are allocated for modules and
external declarations only: types, traits and implementations are matched by
name, and a call finds its operation by name among the visible modules. A
type an extension represents uses one of the binary's closed `Value` forms,
`File` a `Value::Endpoint`, rather than a native value carrying the declared
type's identity. Every extension is compiled in, so binding is per process
rather than per runtime environment.

Steps split by the layering core → graph → knowledge:

| Provider | Steps | How a program gets it |
| --- | --- | --- |
| core | `size` `contains` `get` `count` `sort` `take` `filter` `map` `of_type` `is` `group` `reduce` `sum` `min` `max` | always |
| `graph` | `uplinks` `downlinks` `expand` | the prelude |
| `format` | `format` `text` `markdown` `hmd` `json` `yaml` `csv` `d2` `mermaid` | the prelude |
| `io` | `file` `source` `tap` `target` `sink` | the prelude |
| `net` | `http` `websocket` `socket` | `import net` |
| `logging` | `Logger` `trace` `debug` `info` `warn` `error` | `import logging` |
| `lexical` | `lexical` | `import lexical` |
| `semantic` | `semantic` | `import semantic` |

Collection operations stay in the core because they operate on the core's own
collection types and mention nothing above them. Retrieval and drawing are not
the core's business — see [[lexical]], [[semantic]], [[hql/semantic-search]]
and [[hql/presentation]].

`import <module>` makes a module visible under its last segment or an `as`
alias, `import io.net as net`, and `module.f(…)` is always available for one
that is imported. A builtin module is the extension of that name; a workspace
module is an HQL file under a module root, `hql.toml` `[modules] roots` or
`--modules`. Two visible modules may declare one function name; the input's
type tells them apart at the call, and a call that fits both is refused
naming both ([HQL-0024](../../proposals/HQL-0024/README.md), parts 1 and 2). A vault states its own imports in
`hql.toml`, and may decline the prelude. The rules are in
[the grammar](../../models/behavior/expressions.md) and
[the command line](../../models/behavior/cli.md); the decision is
[HQL-0001](../../proposals/HQL-0001/README.md).

A declaration states whether its operation is pure, `fn`, or effectful,
`effectful fn`, because [[pipes]] reserves execution freedom that is only
safe over pure steps; the extension's own marking is checked against it.
`io`'s `source`, `tap`, `target` and `sink` and `logging`'s five levels are
effectful, and every other step is pure. An operation of a module without a
contract, `graph`, `net`, `lexical` or `semantic`, has no declaration, and
its extension's marking stands. A declaration states the operation's shape
the same way, before `fn` — elementwise, partition, reduction, or a barrier
where nothing is written ([HQL-0004](../../proposals/HQL-0004/README.md),
[[pipes]]) — and an operation without a declaration keeps its extension's
marking, a barrier. The backend a checked program runs on is host
configuration, registered in one table as extensions are; `localhost` is the
only one.

An extension may also contribute metadata while cards are assembled; lexical
owns its own model and indexing entries. Extension-defined types and dynamically
registered document sources are not implemented and need a decision of their own.

An HQL module organizes reusable HQL definitions. An extension connects HQL to
external capabilities: for example, a data source, a renderer or an explicit
persistence operation. These are candidate uses, not a bundled extension list.
An extension may expose pure operations as well as effects.

## Namespace initialization

Namespace initialization is unimplemented and independent of the registry
above: it needs module resolution, which the registry deliberately does not
provide. A vault or namespace may contain `init.hql`. Its intended role is to
import the modules and extensions available to that namespace. This gives the namespace
an explicit entry point for its evaluation environment.

Illustrative layout:

```text
vault/
  init.hql
  alice.hmd
  research/
    init.hql
    notes.hmd
```

Illustrative contents of `init.hql`, using candidate module-import syntax:

```hql
import people
import graph
```

The names represent available modules or extension interfaces; their discovery
and installation are separate host responsibilities. Import syntax, exports,
qualification and any distinction between module and extension imports remain
open. An import is not implicitly a package download.

The research namespace can have its own imports. Whether it inherits the vault's
imports, how names shadow or conflict, initialization order, cycles, reloads and
failure handling all require decisions. The presence of nested `init.hql` files
alone does not settle those policies. Whether initialization permits work beyond
imports is also unresolved.

## Environments and capabilities

Initialization establishes namespace availability. The bridge into card and cell
environments still needs a precise contract. Cells close over their defining card
environment, including explicit imports and bindings. Locals can shadow names;
transclusion preserves the defining closure. Namespace initialization must remain
compatible with those lexical rules. Card filenames never implicitly bind names.

The extension boundary should expose typed inputs, outputs and failures. It also
needs a way to distinguish pure computation from effects so [[pipes]] can be
optimized safely. Importing a capability and invoking an effect are separate
operations. Reading, network access, printing and saving need explicit host
contracts; importing a renderer does not itself render a value.

The host adapter supplies typed HMD documents using the existing parser/resolver.
An extension should operate through that boundary rather than make raw text
parsing part of the core. Native ABI versus another transport, registration,
versioning, resource limits and capability policy remain open. This document does
not choose a Rust plugin ABI or prescribe a trait hierarchy.

See [architecture](../../models/domain/architecture.md),
[cell environments](../../models/behavior/program-values.md) and
[[hql/design-status]].
